# The layers that state a remainder of their own, with the size they put on it. One row per layer
# filing the first branch of the `StatedRemainder` wrapper: the sign, the buffer it names as
# absorber or its reason for naming none, and the quantity if the filer gave one.
#
# The other side of layers/denied_remainders.sqlc
#   It has to be named before the anti-join can be read. Without it, the callers that need
#   `qty_low/mode/high/unit` (arithmetic/filed_remainder.sqlc and matrices.sqlc, through
#   layers/filed_against_derived.sqlc) would reach past both relations into `pm.layer`, and a
#   `WHERE remainder_absent IS NULL` written there is the NOT its sibling already states
#   positively.
#
# Why `qty_unit` is carried
#   The filed remainder is compared with one worked out from the nameplate and the demand, and that
#   comparison needs the unit the filer wrote beside the number. Carrying it here makes the
#   comparison possible at all.
#
# The quantity is itself a typed blank
#   A layer may state that it has a remainder, naming its sign and its absorber, and decline to
#   size it. `qty_absent` is that filing, and it is a different document from one that denies the
#   remainder outright.
-- pm:Layer/pm:remainder taking the pm:claim branch of pm:StatedRemainder.
SELECT l.filing, l.layer,
       l.sign, l.sign_absent,
       l.absorber_taxonomy, l.absorber_value, l.absorber_absent,
       l.qty_low, l.qty_mode, l.qty_high, l.qty_unit, l.qty_absent,
       l.sign_derivation, l.qty_derivation
FROM pm.layer l
WHERE l.remainder_absent IS NULL
